search_informal_description:
Let $R$ be a semiring, and let $M$, $N$, $P$ be additive commutative groups with $R$-module structures. Given linear maps $f \colon M \to N$ and $g \colon N \to P$ forming an exact sequence (i.e., $\operatorname{range} f = \ker g$), the following conditions are equivalent:

1. $f$ is injective and there exists a linear map $l \colon P \to N$ such that $g \circ l = \operatorname{id}_P$;
2. $g$ is surjective and there exists a linear map $l \colon N \to M$ such that $l \circ f = \operatorname{id}_M$;
3. There exists a linear equivalence $e \colon N \simeq M \times P$ such that $f = e^{-1} \circ \operatorname{inl}_{R,M,P}$ and $g = \operatorname{snd}_{R,M,P} \circ e$, where $\operatorname{inl}_{R,M,P} \colon M \to M \times P$ is the left injection map $x \mapsto (x, 0)$ and $\operatorname{snd}_{R,M,P} \colon M \times P \to P$ is the second projection map $(x, y) \mapsto y$.

