search_informal_description:
Let \(f : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\) and \(b\in\mathbb{R}\). Suppose that
1. \(f\) is continuous at \(b\);
2. \(f\) is differentiable at all points in some punctured neighbourhood of \(b\);
3. \(f'(x)\ge 0\) for all \(x\) sufficiently close to \(b\) from the left, and \(f'(x)\le 0\) for all \(x\) sufficiently close to \(b\) from the right.

Then \(f\) has a local maximum at \(b\), i.e. there exists \(\delta>0\) such that \(f(x)\le f(b)\) for all \(x\) with \(|x-b|<\delta\).

