search_informal_description:
For any real number $a \geq 0$, there exists a positive real number $p > 0$ such that the function
\[
t \mapsto F_{\text{nat}}(0, a, t) - \begin{cases} 
1 & \text{if } a = 0, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
is asymptotically bounded by $\exp(-p t)$ as $t \to \infty$. Here $F_{\text{nat}}(0, a, t) = \sum_{n=0}^\infty \exp(-\pi (n + a)^2 t)$.

