search_informal_description:
Let $G$ be a countable group acting on a measurable space $\alpha$ with a finite measure $\mu$. Suppose a set $s \subseteq \alpha$ satisfies the following conditions:
1. $s$ is null-measurable with respect to $\mu$;
2. For every $g \in G$ with $g \neq 1$, the translates $g \cdot s$ and $s$ are almost everywhere disjoint;
3. The action of each $g \in G$ on $\alpha$ is quasi-measure preserving with respect to $\mu$;
4. The total measure of $\alpha$ is bounded by the sum of the measures of all translates of $s$, i.e., $\mu(\alpha) \leq \sum_{g \in G} \mu(g \cdot s)$.

Then $s$ is a fundamental domain for the action of $G$ on $\alpha$ with respect to $\mu$.

