informal_statement:
Let the function \(f(x) = \sin(\omega x+\varphi)\) where \(\omega>0\) and \(|\varphi|<\frac{\pi}{2}\). Four judgments are given:
(1) Its graph is symmetric about the line \(x = \frac{\pi}{12}\);
(2) Its graph is symmetric about the point \((\frac{\pi}{3},0)\);
(3) Its minimum positive period is \(\pi\);
(4) It is an increasing function on the interval \([-\frac{\pi}{6},0]\).

Taking two of these assertions as conditions and the other two as conclusions, the two correct propositions you think are:
Prove that the answer is: \((1)(3)\Rightarrow(2)(4)\) and \((2)(3)\Rightarrow(1)(4)\). If (3) holds, then \(\omega = 2\), so \(f(x)=\sin(2x + \varphi)\). Since (1) holds, when \(x=\frac{\pi}{12}\), \(f(x)\) reaches its maximum or minimum value. That is, \(\sin(\frac{\pi}{6}+\varphi)=\pm1\), and thus \(\varphi=\frac{\pi}{3}\). So \(f(x)=\sin(2x+\frac{\pi}{3})\). Its graph is centrally symmetric about the point \((\frac{\pi}{3},0)\) and it is monotonically increasing on \([-\frac{\pi}{6},0]\).

If (3) holds, then \(\omega = 2\), so \(f(x)=\sin(2x+\varphi)\). Since (2) holds, when \(x = \frac{\pi}{3}\), \(f(x)=0\), that is, \(\sin(\frac{2\pi}{3}+\varphi)=0\). Thus \(\varphi=\frac{\pi}{3}\), so \(f(x)=\sin(2x+\frac{\pi}{3})\), and (1)(4) hold.

